{"type":"delta","text":"Before I answer — opted out of what, exactly? The 40% figure matters a lot, but "}
{"type":"delta","text":"so does what they opted out of: a personalization feature, an AI-assisted "}
{"type":"delta","text":"recommendation, a data-sharing consent flow, something else entirely?\n"}
{"type":"delta","text":"Because the interpretation flips depending on the answer.\n"}
{"type":"delta","text":"If 40% opted out of a data-sharing or personalization consent prompt, that's "}
{"type":"delta","text":"actually not disqualifying on its own. Opt-out rates on consent flows in retail "}
{"type":"delta","text":"run high — often 30-50% — because the prompt design, placement, and framing "}
{"type":"delta","text":"drive the number more than underlying preference. Three or four UX changes can "}
{"type":"delta","text":"move that figure dramatically. You're not measuring product rejection; you're "}
{"type":"delta","text":"measuring prompt design quality.\n"}
{"type":"delta","text":"If 40% actively disengaged from the AI feature itself after experiencing it — "}
{"type":"delta","text":"churned off, turned it off, complained — that's a different signal and I'd take "}
{"type":"delta","text":"it more seriously as a rollout-pause argument.\n"}
{"type":"delta","text":"The other question I'd ask: what did the 60% who stayed in do? If their behavior "}
{"type":"delta","text":"improved materially on the target metric, the math may still favor rollout even "}
{"type":"delta","text":"with the opt-out drag — you're looking at net expected value, not opt-out rate "}
{"type":"delta","text":"in isolation.\n"}
{"type":"delta","text":"What was the feature, and do you have behavioral data on the retained cohort? "}
{"type":"delta","text":"That's what determines whether rolling out is defensible or not."}
{"type":"followups","followups":["What specific behavioral metrics improved for the 60% who stayed in, and by how much? Does that lift outweigh the revenue/engagement loss from the 40% who opted out?","How did opt-out rates vary by user segment (by tenure, usage frequency, demographics)? Were there particular groups that rejected it at much higher rates, suggesting the feature only works for a subset?","What does the opt-out flow itself look like — where is the button, what does the prompt say, and have you A/B tested different framings to see if you can lower the 40% without changing the feature?"]}
{"type":"done"}

